{"name":"Maximum Orange","hex":"#ff5b00","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"maximum","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The highest limit.","The greatest value of a set or other mathematical structure, especially the global maximum or a local maximum of a function."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["To the highest degree."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/maximum"},{"term":"orange","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["An evergreen tree of the genus Citrus such as Citrus sinensis which yields oranges (the fruit).","Any round citrus fruit with a yellow-red colour when ripe and a sour-sweet taste; the fruit of the orange tree.\n Specifically, a sweet orange or Citrus sinensis."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Having the colour of the fruit of an orange tree; yellowred; reddish-yellow."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/orange"}],"entries":[{"title":"Maximum and minimum","blocks":[{"text":"In mathematical analysis, the maximum and minimum of a function are, respectively, the greatest and least value taken by the function. Known generically as extrema, they may be defined either within a given range or on the entire domain of a function. Pierre de Fermat was one of the first mathematicians to propose a general technique, adequality, for finding the maxima and minima of functions."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/68/Extrema_example_original.svg/330px-Extrema_example_original.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Maximum_and_minimum","wikidata":"Q845060"},{"title":"Orange (colour)","blocks":[{"text":"Orange is the colour between yellow and red on the spectrum of visible light. The human eyes perceive orange when observing light with a dominant wavelength between roughly 585 and 620 nanometres. In traditional colour theory, it is a secondary colour of pigments, produced by mixing yellow and red. In the RGB colour model, it is a tertiary colour. It is named after the fruit of the same name."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d8/Delicatearch.png/330px-Delicatearch.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Orange_(colour)","wikidata":"Q39338"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/23/Modell_einer_Peanoschen_Fl%C3%A4che_-Schilling_XLIX%2C_1-.jpg/500px-Modell_einer_Peanoschen_Fl%C3%A4che_-Schilling_XLIX%2C_1-.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/23/Modell_einer_Peanoschen_Fl%C3%A4che_-Schilling_XLIX%2C_1-.jpg/500px-Modell_einer_Peanoschen_Fl%C3%A4che_-Schilling_XLIX%2C_1-.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Peano surface, a counterexample to some criteria of local maxima of the 19th century","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AModell_einer_Peanoschen_Fl%C3%A4che_-Schilling_XLIX%2C_1-.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/62/MaximumParaboloid.png/500px-MaximumParaboloid.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/62/MaximumParaboloid.png/500px-MaximumParaboloid.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The global maximum is the point at the top","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AMaximumParaboloid.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/95/MaximumCounterexample.png/500px-MaximumCounterexample.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/95/MaximumCounterexample.png/500px-MaximumCounterexample.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail